## Types (verbatim)
export type CountFn = (rows: string[]) => number;

## Intent
Exercise "rectangles": implement `count`.

Count the rectangles in an ASCII diagram like the one below.

```text
   +--+
  ++  |
+-++--+
|  |  |
+--+--+
```

The above diagram contains these 6 rectangles:

```text

+-----+
|     |
+-----+
```

```text
   +--+
   |  |
   |  |
   |  |
   +--+
```

```text
   +--+
   |  |
   +--+

```

```text

   +--+
   |  |
   +--+
```

```text

+--+
|  |
+--+
```

```text

  ++
  ++

```

You may assume that the input is always a proper rectangle (i.e. the length of every line equals the length of the first line).

## Examples (deep equality)
{"args":[[]],"expected":0,"name":"Rectangles > no rows"}
{"args":[[""]],"expected":0,"name":"Rectangles > no columns"}
{"args":[[" "]],"expected":0,"name":"Rectangles > no rectangles"}

## Rules
No new imports.
No effects: never use Date.now(), new Date() without arguments, performance, Math.random, crypto, console, process, globalThis, eval, Function, fetch, require, import(), setTimeout or setInterval.

## Your previous attempt failed
Previous body:
const count: CountFn = count;
const n = rows.length;
if (n === 0) return 0;
const m = rows[0].length;
if (m === 0) return 0;
const grid = rows.map(row => row.split(''));
let total = 0;
for (let r1 = 0; r1 < n; r1++) {
    for (let r2 = r1 + 1; r2 < n; r2++) {
      for (let c1 = 0; c1 < m; c1++) {
        for (let c2 = c1 + 1; c2 < m; c2++) {
          if (grid[r1][c1] === '+' && grid[r1][c2] === '+' &&
              grid[r2][c1] === '+' && grid[r2][c2] === '+') {
            let valid = true;
            for (let r = r1 + 1; r < r2; r++) {
              if (grid[r][c1] !== '|' || grid[r][c2] !== '|') {
                valid = false;
                break;
              }
            }
            if (valid) {
              for (let c = c1 + 1; c < c2; c++) {
                if (grid[r1][c] !== '-' || grid[r2][c] !== '-') {
                  valid = false;
                  break;
                }
              }
            }
            if (valid) total++;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
return total;

Problems:
OARC-E070 TS2448 line 39: Block-scoped variable 'count' used before its declaration.
    > const count: CountFn = count;
OARC-E070 TS2454 line 39: Variable 'count' is used before being assigned.
    > const count: CountFn = count;

Fix these problems.

Reply with JSON only: {"body": "<statements>", "imports": []}. Add {"from": "<specifier>", "names": ["<name>"]} to "imports" only for a new import the Rules allow.
